--
N5008 [expr.assign]/1: a compound assignment is an lvalue referring to its
left operand, so `(x += 2) += 3' with x a reference to b adds 5 to b.  Goto
conversion follows C, where the result of an assignment is its value: with
a left operand that is not a plain variable (here *x) the inner result was
a temporary, and the outer compound assignment updated only the temporary
(b == 3, bin53).  The C++ front end now uses such a nested side effect as
`*&(x += 2)' (also for ++(++x) and (x = 2) = 3), and goto conversion cleans
the operands of a side effect whose address is taken.
test_parameter.desc: the same through a reference parameter, whose return
binds a reference to the result, aborted symbolic execution
("goto_symext::address_arithmetic does not handle side_effect").  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 accept both programs, and they fail only their WRONG assertion.
